_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{2x})^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ (-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{e}^{5x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.$" 13194139535934 true] [17592186147716 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375195 13194139535934 