s case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{x})}^{′}+{({u}_{2}{e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} & ={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} & ={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+\\cancel{{u}_{1}{e}^{x}}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}} & =\\cancel{{u}_{1}{e}^{x}}+\\cancel{2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0.\n$$" 13194139535934 true] [17592186147718 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535934 true] [17592186147718 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{″} - 3y^{′} + 2y = x^{3}e^{5x}$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_{1}(x) = e^{x}$ and $y_{2} = e^{2x}$.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution\n$y_{p}(x) = u_{1}(x)y_{1} + u_{2}(x)y_{2}$\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n${u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = x^{3}e^{5x}; u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0$\n\nGiven that $u_{1}(x) = - ∫g(x)dx$, find the function $g$." 13194139535934 false] [17592186147718 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" 13194139535934 true] [17592186147718 1145 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.$" 13194139535934 true] [17592186375195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375196 13194139535934 true] [17592186375195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375197 13194139535934 true] [17592186375195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375198 13194139535934 true] [17592186375195 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535934 true] [17592186375195 1041 "field-3" 13194139535934 true] [17592186375195 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375197 13194139535934 true] [17592186375195 1110 #uuid "edc81094-026b-539a-9900-9417504fd7ff" 13194139535934 true] [17592186375196 1034 "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}" 13194139535934 true] [17592186375196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535934 true] [17592186375196 1111 #uuid "73c1cbc7-3279-5ccb-ace7-12d508f30069" 13194139535934 true] [17592186375197 1034 "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}" 13194139535934 true] [17592186375197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535934 true] [17592186375197 1111 #uuid "3deb4c1e-9926-54e1-810b-efb2cc582d97" 13194139535934 true] [17592186375198 1034 "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{